## Tutorials

When you know exactly how many times you want to loop through a block of 
code, use theforloop instead of awhileloop:

Statement 1is executed (one time) before the execution of the code block.

Statement 2defines the condition for executing the code block.

Statement 3is executed (every time) after the code block has been executed.

The example below will print the numbers 0 to 4:

Example explained

This example prints even values between 0 and 10:

This example calculates the sum of numbers from 1 to 5:

This example prints a countdown from 5 to 1:

### Code Examples

```
for (
statement 1
;
statement 2
;
statement 3
) {
// code block to be executed
}

---

for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
cout << i << "\n";
}

---

for (int i = 0; i <= 10; i = i + 2) {
cout << i << "\n";
}

---

int sum = 0;
for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
sum = sum + i;
}
cout << "Sum is " << sum;

---

for (int i = 5; i > 0; i--) {
cout << i << "\n";
}
```

## Tutorials

It is also possible to place a loop inside another loop. This is called anested loop.

The "inner loop" will be executed one time for each iteration of the "outer loop":

This example uses nested loops to print a simple multiplication table (1 to 3):

Nested loops are useful when working with tables, matrices, ormulti-dimensional data structures.

### Code Examples

```
// Outer loop
for (int i = 1; i <= 2; ++i) {
cout << "Outer: " << i << "\n"; // Executes 2 times
// Inner 
  loop
for (int j = 1; j <= 3; ++j) {
cout << " 
  Inner: " << j << "\n"; // Executes 6 times (2 * 3)
}
}

---

for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
for (int j = 1; j <= 3; j++) {
cout << i * j << " ";
}
cout << "\n";
}

---

1 2 3
2 4 6
3 6 9
```

## Tutorials

There is also a "for-eachloop" (also known as ranged-based for loop), which is 
used to loop through elements in anarray(or otherdata structures):

The following example outputs all elements in an array, using a "for-eachloop":

You can also use a for-each loop to loop through characters in a string:

### Code Examples

```
for (
type variableName
:
arrayName
) {
// code block to be executed
}

---

int myNumbers[5] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};
for (int num : myNumbers) {
cout << num << "\n";
}

---

string word = "Hello";
for (char c : word) {
cout << c << "\n";
}
```

